<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 5: Visualising Multidimensional Data                     -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Visualising Multidimensional Data</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-visualising-multidimensional">
    <p>
      <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> was all about getting to a point where
      you can trust your data.
      This chapter is about actually <em>seeing</em> it.
      Even once a dataset is clean and correctly coded, if it has more than two
      or three variables, it's still just a table.
      And honestly, a table with more than a few hundred rows tells a person
      almost nothing!
      As soon as a dataset has some real structure to it (groups, locations,
      time, several measurements for each case), the question changes from <q>is
      this value right?</q> to <q>how do I look at several things at once?</q>
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: Following the Giants                                  -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-following-the-giants">
    <title>Following the Giants</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        What does twenty years of elephant movement look like as data?
        Let's have a look.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gathuku-elephant-gps">
        <caption>
          GPS relocations from 110 collared elephants collected from 2001 to
          2021 in the Laikipia-Samburu ecosystem, Kenya, overlaid on land use
          boundaries and major water courses.
          Each dot represents a single GPS fix; the density and spread of points
          reveals the elephants' collective use of the landscape over two
          decades.
          Reproduced unchanged from Gathuku and Wittemyer (2025),
          <em>Landscape Ecology</em>, 40, 212,
          <url href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10980-025-02222-w"/>, under CC
          BY-NC-ND 4.0.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/gathuku2025-fig1b.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A map of northern Kenya showing thousands of orange GPS location
              points from 110 collared elephants overlaid on a background
              showing land use boundaries in different colours and rivers in
              blue.
              The density and distribution of points reveals the collective home
              range of the tracked population across a landscape spanning
              national parks, conservancies, private ranches, community
              conservancies, and agricultural settlements.
              The concentration of points is highest in the central part of the
              study area, thinning toward the edges.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Each orange dot on this map is one GPS location, recorded by a collar
        fitted to one of 110 African savanna elephants tracked across the
        Laikipia-Samburu ecosystem in northern Kenya.
        There are 3,850,840 of them, collected over twenty years, between 2001
        and 2021.
        And before anyone has done any statistical analysis at all, this map
        already shows us where the elephants went, as a group, across a
        landscape of national parks, private ranches, community conservancies,
        and farmland.
        You can see where the dots are concentrated, where the gaps are, and the
        corridors along the rivers, all at a glance.
        No table of GPS coordinates could ever show you that.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the dataset at the heart of Gathuku and Wittemyer's (2025) study
        of what drives how far elephants range, and how they move, in northern
        Kenya.
        We think it's a brilliant place to start thinking about why
        visualisation matters so much once your data has more than one or two
        variables (or <q>dimensions</q>).
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-elephants-dimensions">
      <title>More than just location</title>

      <p>
        Nobody is going to scroll through 3,850,840 rows looking for a pattern!
        At that scale, a spreadsheet is somewhere to store data, and you can't
        really use it to look at the data.
        And the GPS coordinates are only part of the story.
        Each location was also linked to information about the individual
        elephant, like its sex and identity.
        It was also linked to measurements of the environment around that
        location, such as:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              how much vegetation was growing there (measured as NDVI, a
              satellite-derived index of greenness)
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              how much rainfall the area received and how variable that rainfall
              was
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              how much surface water and permanent water was available nearby
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              how rugged the terrain was
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              how much human activity had modified the landscape (e.g.,
              settlements, agriculture, and infrastructure).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        If you want to understand what drives how much space an elephant uses,
        and whether it keeps coming back to the same areas over time, you need
        to look at all of these variables (or dimensions) together, because one
        at a time won't cut it.
        A table with 3,850,840 rows and a dozen columns isn't going to answer
        that question for you, but visualisation is a really good place to
        start.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-elephants-findings">
      <title>What the data showed</title>

      <p>
        So what did they find?
        When Gathuku and Wittemyer (2025) looked at home range size alongside
        the landscape variables, water came out as the biggest factor.
        Where water was scarce or unpredictable, elephants ranged over larger
        areas.
        Where there was plenty of surface and permanent water, they stayed
        within smaller areas.
        That makes a lot of ecological sense: if you know where the water is,
        there's no need to travel far, and if you don't, you have to go and find
        it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Rainfall variability had the same effect.
        The more unpredictable the rainfall, the bigger the elephants' yearly
        ranges, as they travelled further to track resources that were spread
        unevenly across the landscape.
      </p>

      <p>
        The effect of people on the landscape was more complicated.
        In areas with more human activity, elephants tended to shift their
        ranges more from one period to the next, while still staying within a
        restricted overall area.
        So they were moving around a lot inside a smaller space, instead of
        ranging freely.
        This fits with elephants responding to the risks that people bring, and
        changing how they move to avoid danger, even if it means missing out on
        better resources.
      </p>

      <p>
        There were clear differences between the sexes too.
        Females tended to be more faithful to their ranges over time, coming
        back to the same areas year after year more consistently than males.
        This probably reflects the more stable social structure of female-led
        family groups, who rely on what they've learned over the years about
        where to find reliable water and food in a familiar area.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two of the figures in the paper show these patterns in ways that summary
        statistics on their own just can't.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gathuku-violin-annual">
        <caption>
          Annual home range estimates for female (red) and male (blue) elephants
          in the Laikipia-Samburu ecosystem, shown for two estimation methods
          and two range definitions.
          The wide, right-skewed distributions reveal enormous variability
          between individuals.
          Reproduced unchanged from Gathuku and Wittemyer (2025),
          <em>Landscape Ecology</em>, 40, 212,
          <url href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10980-025-02222-w"/>, under CC
          BY-NC-ND 4.0.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/gathuku2025-fig8.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Four violin plots arranged in a two-by-two grid.
              The left column shows 50% isopleth home range estimates and the
              right shows 95% isopleth estimates.
              Within each panel, female estimates are shown in red and male in
              blue, side by side, for both AKDE and MCP estimation methods.
              The distributions are wide and right-skewed for both sexes,
              indicating high individual variability.
              Female and male distributions overlap substantially.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The first of these, <xref ref="fig-gathuku-violin-annual"/>, shows home
        range sizes for female and male elephants.
        You'll notice it has two panels.
        These show two different definitions of what counts as the home range:
        the 50% isopleth (roughly, the core area an elephant uses most) and the
        95% isopleth (the wider area it ranges over).
        And within each panel, there are two different mathematical methods for
        estimating home range size (AKDE and MCP).
        Don't worry about the details of those methods here.
        What we want you to notice is what the figure is doing.
        It shows four variables at once: home range size on the vertical axis,
        the range definition in the columns, the estimation method within each
        panel, and sex in the colour.
        Each shape is called a violin plot, and it shows the full spread of
        individual estimates, as well as giving you a sense of the average.
        You can see straight away that some elephants range over huge areas,
        while others stay really local.
        And you can see that this pattern holds for both sexes, both range
        definitions, and both methods.
        Now imagine this as a table of means and standard deviations instead.
        That table would give you the averages, but it wouldn't show you the
        shape of the distribution, or how much the sexes overlap.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gathuku-violin-overlap">
        <caption>
          Home range fidelity (overlap index) across annual and seasonal scales
          (left panel) and by sex at both scales (right panel).
          Higher values indicate greater overlap between successive home ranges
          (that is, stronger site fidelity).
          Reproduced unchanged from Gathuku and Wittemyer (2025),
          <em>Landscape Ecology</em>, 40, 212,
          <url href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10980-025-02222-w"/>, under CC
          BY-NC-ND 4.0.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/gathuku2025-fig4.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Two violin plots side by side.
              The left panel shows UDOI values for annual and seasonal scales,
              with annual overlap (tan/orange violin) tending to be higher than
              seasonal overlap (blue violin), indicating stronger fidelity at
              the annual scale.
              The right panel shows UDOI values split by sex at both annual and
              seasonal scales, with four violins: female annual (red), male
              annual (blue), female seasonal (red), and male seasonal (blue).
              Females tend to show slightly higher overlap values than males,
              particularly at the annual scale, consistent with the more stable
              ranging behaviour of female-led family groups.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The second figure adds a different dimension: consistency over time.
        Instead of asking where an elephant went, it asks: <q>how much did the
        same elephant's range in one period overlap with its range in the
        next?</q>
        An index close to 1 means the elephant used almost exactly the same
        area, and close to 0 means it moved somewhere quite different.
        Again, don't worry about the technical details of how this index is
        calculated.
        The important thing is that the figure shows three things at once: the
        scale of the comparison (annual or seasonal) on the horizontal axis, how
        much the ranges overlap on the vertical axis, and sex in the colour.
        So you can see immediately that elephants are generally pretty faithful
        to their ranges from year to year, and that females are a little more
        faithful than males, at both scales.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-elephants-lesson">
        <title>The same data, seen together</title>

        <p>
          Water drives range size, human activity restricts movement, and
          females are more faithful to their ranges than males.
          But you can't see any of these findings in a single column of the
          dataset, or even in the GPS locations on their own.
          Each pattern only shows up once you look at location <em>together</em>
          with the landscape variables, sex, season, and time, in the same
          visualisation.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <table xml:id="table-elephant-dimensions">
        <title>
          What had to be held in view at once to find these patterns
        </title>

        <tabular halign="left">
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Held in view</cell>
            <cell>Variable(s)</cell>
            <cell>What it contributed</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Position</cell>
            <cell>Latitude, longitude</cell>
            <cell>Where each elephant actually was</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Water</cell>
            <cell>Surface water, permanent water, rainfall variability</cell>
            <cell>The dominant driver of range size</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Human pressure</cell>
            <cell>Human modification index</cell>
            <cell>Why elephants shift more but range less freely</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Individual</cell>
            <cell>Sex</cell>
            <cell>Where the sharpest differences in fidelity appeared</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Time</cell>
            <cell>Year, season</cell>
            <cell>How consistent ranging behaviour was over time</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        That's five things held in view together, and none of them needed any
        new data collection.
        We'll see exactly the same idea in <xref ref="sec-gapminder"/>, where we
        add dimensions to a plot one at a time.
      </p>

      <p>
        In this chapter, you'll learn how to build exactly that kind of view:
        visualisations that show you several variables at once, instead of one
        table or one chart for each variable.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Gathuku, N.M.
        and Wittemyer, G.
        (2025).
        <q>Identification of key landscape drivers of range size and 
      range shifts in African savanna elephants in northern Kenya.</q>
        <em>Landscape Ecology</em>, 40, 212.
        <url href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10980-025-02222-w"/>.
        Licensed under CC BY-NC-ND 4.0.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 2: a short gallery                                       -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-viz-gallery">
    <title>Seeing More: Three Charts That Changed Minds</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The elephant study needed computers, GPS satellites, and twenty years of
        fieldwork.
        But the idea that some questions can only be answered by showing several
        things at once is much, much older.
        Long before computers existed, people kept running into the same
        problem: a table of numbers that held the answer but couldn't show it,
        and a simple chart that showed something, but not enough.
      </p>

      <p>
        The three examples below all go back more than 150 years, and we love
        them.
        Each one was drawn by hand, in ink, on paper.
        Each one changed how people thought about something really important: a
        military disaster, a public health crisis, and a disease outbreak.
        And each one worked for exactly the same reason.
        It showed something that had been in the data all along, but that nobody
        could see until someone found the right way to draw it.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-viz-minard">
      <title>Minard's map of Napoleon's Russian campaign (1869)</title>

      <p>
        Charles Joseph Minard was a retired French civil engineer, 88 years old,
        when he published a single lithograph that Edward Tufte later called
        <q>possibly the best statistical graphic ever drawn.</q>
        It shows Napoleon's disastrous invasion of Russia in 1812, and the
        retreat that followed.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-minard">
        <caption>
          Charles Joseph Minard,
          <em>Carte figurative des pertes successives 
      en hommes de l'Armée Française dans la campagne de Russie 
      1812<ndash/>1813</em>
          (1869).
          The width of each band represents troop numbers at one millimetre per
          ten thousand men; the lower strip shows temperature along the retreat
          route.
          Public domain.
          Image via
          <url href="https://flowingdata.com/2008/07/17/is-minards-map-of-napoleons-march-the-greatest-statistical-graphic-ever/">FlowingData</url>.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_minard_map.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A large horizontal graphic in two parts.
              The upper part shows a map of the route from the Polish border to
              Moscow and back, with a wide tan band tracing the army's advance
              eastward and a thinner black band tracing the retreat westward.
              Numbers printed along the bands show troop counts at key points:
              the tan band begins at 422,000 near the Polish border and narrows
              steadily; the black retreat band thins dramatically, reaching
              around 10,000 survivors by the time it crosses back into Poland.
              Town names mark the route.
              The lower part of the graphic shows a temperature chart aligned
              with the retreat route, with temperatures falling as low as minus
              30 degrees at the worst points of the retreat.
              Dates are marked along the bottom.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        If you tried to show this with bar charts of monthly troop losses, you'd
        need several separate charts: one for the size of the army, one for
        where it was, one for which way it was going, and one for the
        temperature that killed so many soldiers on the retreat.
        Minard fitted all of it into one image.
        A tan band traces the army's advance east towards Moscow, and its width
        is scaled at exactly one millimetre per ten thousand men.
        It's drawn straight onto a map, so you see where the army was and how
        big it was at the same time.
        A black band, on the same scale, traces the retreat.
        And underneath the map, a separate strip plots the temperature against
        the date along the retreat route, so you can read the collapse in troop
        numbers directly against the cold that caused so much of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's six variables in one image: geography, direction of travel, troop
        numbers, the route itself, temperature, and time.
        The tan band starts near the Polish border with 422,000 men, and narrows
        steadily as the army marches into Russia, losing soldiers to disease and
        fighting even before it reaches Moscow.
        The retreat band is where it gets really devastating.
        It shrinks almost to nothing by the time it reaches that same border,
        months later, with around 10,000 survivors, while the temperature strip
        underneath falls below <m>-30^\circ\text{C}</m> at its worst.
      </p>

      <p>
        A table of monthly casualty figures had the same information in it, and
        had been around for decades.
        But Minard made it visible to anyone who looked.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Minard, C.J.
        (1869).
        <em>Carte figurative des pertes successives en hommes de l'Armée 
    Française dans la campagne de Russie 1812<ndash/>1813.</em>
        Described in Tufte, E.R.
        (1983).
        <em>The Visual Display of Quantitative Information.</em> Cheshire, CT:
        Graphics Press.
        Public domain.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-viz-nightingale">
      <title>Nightingale's Coxcomb (1858)</title>

      <p>
        In 1858, Florence Nightingale published a report built almost entirely
        around one fact that she needed British officials to stop ignoring.
        Most of the soldiers who died in the Crimean War didn't die in battle.
        They died of preventable disease, in military hospitals.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-nightingale">
        <caption>
          Florence Nightingale,
          <em>Diagram of the Causes of Mortality in the 
      Army in the East</em>,
          1858.
          Two polar area diagrams showing monthly deaths from preventable
          disease (blue-grey), wounds in battle (pink/red), and other causes
          (black), for April 1854 to March 1855 (right) and April 1855 to March
          1856 (left).
          Wedge area is proportional to deaths.
          Public domain.
          Image via
          <url href="https://www.historyofinformation.com/image.php?id=851">History of Information</url>.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_nightingale.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Two circular polar area diagrams side by side, titled
              <q>Diagram of the Causes of Mortality in the Army in the East.</q>
              The right diagram covers April 1854 to March 1855, with twelve
              wedges arranged clockwise from April 1854.
              The wedges for the winter months (particularly January and
              February 1855) are dramatically large, dominated by the blue-grey
              sections representing deaths from preventable disease.
              The pink/red sections representing deaths from wounds in battle
              are small by comparison throughout.
              The left diagram covers April 1855 to March 1856, after sanitary
              reforms were introduced.
              The wedges are initially large, but over time the blue-grey
              disease sections have shrunk to a fraction of their earlier size.
              A dashed line connects the two diagrams at the point of transition
              between the two periods to illustrate the scale, so you can see
              directly how much the wedges have shrunk.
              A legend in italic script at the lower left explains that blue
              wedges represent preventable or mitigable zymotic diseases, red
              wedges represent deaths from wounds, and black wedges represent
              all other causes.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Nightingale could have just reported a single mortality rate.
        Instead, she built what became known as the <term>coxcomb</term>, or
        rose diagram: two circular charts, one for each year of the war, each
        split into twelve monthly wedges.
        The area of each wedge was scaled to the number of deaths that month,
        and each wedge was split by colour into three causes: preventable
        disease, wounds from battle, and everything else.
        Just like Minard's map, the power here comes from a pattern developing
        over time, and you can only see it because time, cause, and the number
        of deaths are all shown in the same image.
      </p>

      <p>
        And the pattern is stark.
        Start with the diagram on the right, which shows the winter of 1854 to
        1855, before the Sanitary Commission arrived to investigate conditions
        in the military hospitals.
        The wedges for January and February balloon outwards, and they're mostly
        blue-grey: deaths from disease.
        It later came out that the hospital had been built on top of a sewer,
        and patients were drinking contaminated water.
        Now look at the diagram on the left, for the following year, after the
        Commission had flushed out the hospitals and improved the ventilation.
        The dashed line joining the two diagrams keeps the same scale, so you
        can see directly how much the wedges have shrunk.
        The death rate in the hospitals fell from around 42% to around 2%.
      </p>

      <p>
        Nightingale was a trained statistician, trying to persuade a government
        that had shown very little interest in reading tables of army death
        rates.
        Her diagram is credited with directly influencing the sanitary reforms
        that followed, both in the military and more widely.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Nightingale, F.
        (1858).
        <em>Notes on Matters Affecting the Health, Efficiency, and Hospital 
    Administration of the British Army, Founded Chiefly on the Experience 
    of the Late War.</em>
        London: Harrison and Sons.
        Public domain.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-viz-snow">
      <title>John Snow's cholera map (1854)</title>

      <p>
        In late August 1854, cholera broke out in Soho, London, and killed over
        600 people in just a few weeks.
        At the time, most people believed cholera spread through bad air
        (<q>miasma</q>), rather than through water.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-snow">
        <caption>
          John Snow's map of cholera deaths in the Soho district of London,
          1854.
          Each dot marks a cholera death at the address where it occurred.
          P marks local water pumps; the filled circle marks the contaminated
          Broad Street pump.
          The clustering of deaths around a single pump, and their thinning with
          distance from it, made visible a pattern that a table of addresses and
          death counts could not.
          Public domain.
          Image via
          <url href="https://www.york.ac.uk/depts/maths/histstat/snow_map.htm">University of York</url>.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch4_snow.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A black and white street map of the Soho district of London,
              oriented with north to the upper right.
              Streets are labelled, including Oxford Street at the top, Broad
              Street in the centre, and Piccadilly at the bottom.
              Small dots are scattered across the map, each representing a
              cholera death at a specific address.
              The dots cluster densely around the intersection of Broad Street
              and Cambridge Street in the centre of the map, with density
              falling off with distance in all directions.
              Several pump locations are marked with the letter P around the
              neighbourhood.
              A larger filled circle marks the contaminated Broad Street pump,
              sitting at the centre of the densest cluster of deaths.
              Areas further from the Broad Street pump, including the southern
              portion of the map toward Piccadilly, have very few or no dots at
              all.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The physician John Snow suspected they were wrong.
        So he tested his idea by taking a street map of the neighbourhood,
        marking a dot for every recorded death at the address where it happened,
        and marking every local water pump.
        The map showed a clear pattern: deaths clustered around one pump, on
        Broad Street, and thinned out the further away you got from it.
        Meanwhile, households with their own water supply had almost no cases,
        and neither did a nearby brewery whose workers drank hardly any of the
        local water.
      </p>

      <p>
        You could have found the same information in a table of addresses and
        death counts.
        But the map shows you how close each death was to each pump, and that's
        what made the difference.
        Snow presented it to the local parish council, and the handle was taken
        off the Broad Street pump.
        The outbreak was already slowing down by then, so removing the handle
        didn't end it on its own.
        But the map gave the first clear evidence that cholera spread through
        contaminated water, and not through the air.
        That was later confirmed when an inspection found that the pump's water
        supply was right next to a leaking cesspit.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Snow, J.
        (1855).
        <em>On the Mode of Communication of Cholera</em> (2nd ed.).
        London: John Churchill.
        Public domain.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
