  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 4: Gapminder                                             -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-gapminder">
    <title>Building Up Dimensions in R</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Hans Rosling's Gapminder data makes the same point as the elephants, but
        with a dataset that's small enough to really get your hands on.
        The data is completely open, and a cleaned extract comes ready to use in
        R, in the <c>gapminder</c> package.
        It has 1,704 observations, covering 142 countries from 1952 to 2007,
        with one row per country per year.
        The columns are <c>country</c>, <c>continent</c>, <c>year</c>,
        <c>lifeExp</c> (life expectancy at birth, in years), <c>pop</c>
        (population), and <c>gdpPercap</c> (GDP per capita, in
        inflation-adjusted US dollars).
      </p>

      <p>
        On its own, a single row doesn't tell you anything very interesting.
        But you can build this data up, one variable (or dimension) at a time,
        into a single animated chart, and that's exactly what we're going to do.
        We'll start with a blank canvas, and add one dimension at a time.
      </p>

      <p>
        First, install and load the packages we need:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # Note that you may not need to run these install.packages lines if you already have them installed.
        install.packages("gapminder")
        install.packages("ggplot2")
        install.packages("dplyr")

        library(gapminder)
        library(ggplot2)
        library(dplyr)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Before we start plotting, it's worth taking a moment to understand
        what's actually in the dataset.
        In R, <c>?gapminder</c> opens the documentation for the package:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    ?gapminder
        </code>
      </program>
      <pre>
    Excerpt of the Gapminder data on life expectancy, GDP per capita,
    and population by country.

    Format
    The main data frame gapminder has 1704 rows and 6 variables:

    country     factor with 142 levels
    continent   factor with 5 levels
    year        ranges from 1952 to 2007 in increments of 5 years
    lifeExp     life expectancy at birth, in years
    pop         population
    gdpPercap   GDP per capita (US$, inflation-adjusted)
      </pre>
      <p>
        There are a few things worth noticing before we start.
        <c>country</c> and <c>continent</c> are both factors.
        These are categorical variables with a fixed set of levels, which we
        look at properly in <xref ref="ch-handling-factors"/>.
        <c>year</c> isn't continuous: it jumps in five-year steps, so it goes
        1952, 1957, 1962, and so on.
        <c>gdpPercap</c> is inflation-adjusted, which means you can compare
        values from different years in real terms.
        And <c>pop</c> is a raw count, which, as we'll see, varies so hugely
        between countries that it needs a bit of care to show clearly in a plot.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let's start with just one year, 2007.
        To do this, we filter the dataset:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        gapminder_2007 &lt;- gapminder %&gt;%
              dplyr::filter(year == 2007)

        dim(gapminder_2007)
        #&gt; [1] 142   6

        head(gapminder_2007)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 x 6
        #&gt;   country     continent  year lifeExp      pop gdpPercap
        #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;       &lt;fct&gt;     &lt;int&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;int&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
        #&gt; 1 Afghanistan Asia       2007    43.8 31889923      975.
        #&gt; 2 Albania     Europe     2007    76.4  3600523     5937.
        #&gt; 3 Algeria     Africa     2007    72.3 33333216     6223.
        #&gt; 4 Angola      Africa     2007    42.7 12420476     4797.
        #&gt; 5 Argentina   Americas   2007    75.3 40301927    12779.
        #&gt; 6 Australia   Oceania    2007    81.2 20434176    34435.
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        There are six variables, and one country per row.
        The first row tells us that in 2007, Afghanistan had a life expectancy
        of 43.8 years, a population of 31 million, and a GDP per capita of $975.
        That tells us something, but not much.
        It gets much more interesting when we look at how all 142 countries
        compare with each other.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-2d">
      <title>Two dimensions: does money buy a longer life?</title>

      <p>
        Let's start with the most basic question we can ask: is a country's
        income related to how long its people live?
        <c>ggplot2</c> uses a system called the <q>Grammar of Graphics</q>,
        where you build a plot up in layers.
        The first layer is always the same.
        You tell R which dataset to use, and which variables go with which
        visual properties.
        These are called the <term>aesthetic mappings</term>.
        Here, we put <c>gdpPercap</c> on the x-axis, and <c>lifeExp</c> on the
        y-axis.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp))
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-2d-empty">
        <caption>
          A blank plot, with <c>gdpPercap</c> on the x axis and <c>lifeExp</c>
          on the y axis.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_2d_empty.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A blank rectangle, with <c>gdpPercap</c> on the x axis and
              <c>lifeExp</c> on the y axis.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        This gives you a blank canvas, with the axes set up but nothing drawn on
        it yet.
        To actually draw something, you add a <term>geom</term>.
        That's short for geometric object, and it's the shape used to show each
        observation.
        <c>geom_point()</c> draws one point for each row, which gives us a
        scatterplot.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp)) +
          geom_point()
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-2d">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita against life expectancy for 142 countries in 2007.
          The relationship is positive but curved: richer countries tend to
          have longer life expectancies, but with diminishing returns at higher
          incomes.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_2d.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A scatterplot with GDP per capita on the x-axis and life
              expectancy in years on the y-axis.
              Points are clustered toward the lower left for poorer countries
              with lower life expectancies, and spread toward the upper right
              for wealthier countries.
              The relationship is curved rather than linear, with a steep rise
              at low income levels that flattens out at higher incomes.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        There's a clear positive relationship, but the x-axis is a bit awkward.
        A handful of very rich countries are spread out far to the right, which
        squashes all the other points into a dense cluster on the left.
        This is a right-skewed distribution: most countries are bunched together
        at lower incomes, with a long tail of a few rich ones.
        A log scale fixes this by spreading out the lower end and squashing the
        upper end, so the pattern across the whole range becomes much easier to
        read.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can also add another layer, <c>labs()</c>, to give our axes clearer
        labels than the default variable names.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp)) +
          geom_point() +
          scale_x_log10() +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-2d-log">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita (log scale) against life expectancy for 142 countries
          in 2007.
          The log scale spreads the x-axis more evenly, making the positive
          relationship across all income levels clearer.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_2d_log.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A scatterplot with GDP per capita on a log scale on the x-axis and
              life expectancy in years on the y-axis.
              The points now spread more evenly across the x-axis, and a roughly
              linear positive relationship is visible across the full range.
              Axis labels read GDP per capita (log scale) and Life expectancy.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        So we have two dimensions, because we have two variables, and one clear
        trend.
        But every point is the same colour and the same size.
        A point for Norway and a point for Kenya look exactly the same, apart
        from where they are on the plot.
        We've used the x-axis and the y-axis, and that's it.
        There's a lot more in the data that we can use to make this plot
        clearer, and to explore the relationships further.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-3d">
      <title>Three dimensions: adding continent</title>

      <p>
        We can add a third variable without adding a new axis.
        Instead of position, we use colour.
        If we map <c>continent</c> onto <c>colour</c>, each continent's points
        get a different colour.
        Because this is a new dimension, it goes inside <c>aes()</c>.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp, 
                                   colour = continent)) +
          geom_point() +
          scale_x_log10() +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-3d">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita (log scale) against life expectancy for 142 countries
          in 2007, coloured by continent.
          African countries cluster toward the lower left; European and Oceanian
          countries toward the upper right.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_3d.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A scatterplot with the same axes as before.
              Points are now coloured by continent. African points (red)
              cluster toward the lower left of the chart, European and Oceanian
              points cluster toward the upper right, and American and Asian
              points are spread across the middle.
              A colour legend identifies each continent.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The overall trend is still there, but now we can see some structure in
        it.
        African countries cluster towards the lower left, European countries
        towards the upper right, and for the first time, we can see the spread
        within each continent.
        Nothing about the data has changed.
        We've just added one aesthetic mapping!
      </p>

      <p>
        It's also really important to think about accessibility.
        Colour on its own can be hard to tell apart for readers who are
        colourblind.
        If we add <c>shape = continent</c> as well as <c>colour = continent</c>,
        each continent gets its own colour <em>and</em> its own point shape.
        That way, the chart still works when it's printed in black and white, or
        when it's read by someone with colour blindness.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp, 
                                   colour = continent,
                                   shape = continent)) +
          geom_point() +
          scale_x_log10() +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-3d-2">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita (log scale) against life expectancy for 142 countries
          in 2007.
          Each point has its own colour and shape per continent.
          African countries cluster toward the lower left; European and Oceanian
          countries toward the upper right.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_3d_2.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A scatterplot with the same axes as before.
              Points are now coloured by continent. African points (red,
              circle) cluster toward the lower left of the chart, European and
              Oceanian points cluster toward the upper right, and American and
              Asian points are spread across the middle.
              A colour and shape legend identifies each continent.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Notice that <c>colour</c> and <c>shape</c> are both mapped to the same
        variable, <c>continent</c>.
        So they don't add a new dimension, but they do make the third dimension
        easier for everyone to read.
        We'll keep both from here on.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-4d">
      <title>Four dimensions: adding population</title>

      <p>
        We can add a fourth variable using point size.
        If we map <c>pop</c> onto <c>size</c>, each point's area is proportional
        to the country's population, and our scatterplot becomes a bubble chart.
        We also add <c>alpha = 0.7</c> inside <c>geom_point()</c>.
        <c>alpha</c> controls how transparent the points are, where 1 is
        completely solid and 0 is invisible.
        At 0.7, you can still see overlapping points through each other, which
        really helps when big bubbles sit on top of smaller ones.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp,
                                   colour = continent, shape = continent,
                                   size = pop)) +
          geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
          scale_x_log10() +
          scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 15)) +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy",
               size = "Population")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-4d">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita (log scale) against life expectancy for 142 countries
          in 2007, with point size proportional to population and colour and
          shape indicating continent.
          China and India dominate by size; Kenya sits in the African cluster
          toward the lower left.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_4d.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bubble chart with the same axes and colour/shape encoding as
              before.
              Point sizes now vary substantially: two very large points (China
              and India) are visible in the Asian cluster in the middle of the
              chart.
              Most other points are considerably smaller.
              A size legend shows population scale.
              African points remain clustered toward the lower left.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        China and India have similar incomes and life expectancies to several
        smaller countries, but now they really stand out, just because of their
        size.
        But where's Kenya?
        The African cluster is dense, and it's hard to pick out individual
        countries.
        So let's add labels for a few countries we're interested in.
        This is a really useful general trick for drawing attention to
        particular points, without labelling everything.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        library(dplyr)

        # Countries to label
        label_countries &lt;- c("Kenya", "Nigeria", "South Africa", 
                              "Ethiopia", "China", "India")

        gapminder_labels &lt;- gapminder_2007 %&gt;%
          filter(country %in% label_countries)

        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp,
                                   colour = continent, shape = continent,
                                   size = pop)) +
          geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
          geom_text(data = gapminder_labels, 
                    aes(label = country), 
                    size = 3, nudge_y = 1, show.legend = FALSE) +
          scale_x_log10() +
          scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 10)) +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy",
               size = "Population")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-4d-labels">
        <caption>
          The same chart as above with selected countries labelled, including
          Kenya, Nigeria, South Africa, Ethiopia, China, and India.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_4d_labels.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bubble chart as before, with text labels added for Kenya,
              Nigeria, South Africa, Ethiopia, China, and India.
              Kenya appears in the African cluster toward the lower left of the
              chart.
              China and India are labelled near the large bubbles in the Asian
              cluster.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-5d">
      <title>Five dimensions: adding time</title>

      <p>
        So far, our chart is a single snapshot of 2007.
        Rosling's original chart was animated across every year in the data, so
        you could watch countries move as their income and life expectancy
        changed.
        We can show that same fifth dimension, time, as small multiples, using
        <c>facet_wrap()</c>.
        This makes one panel for each year, and lays them out in a grid.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp,
                              colour = continent, shape = continent,
                              size = pop)) +
          geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
          scale_x_log10() +
          scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 10)) +
          facet_wrap(~ year) +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy",
               size = "Population")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-5d">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita (log scale) against life expectancy for 142 countries,
          faceted by year from 1952 to 2007.
          Read left to right, top to bottom: life expectancy climbs steadily in
          almost every country, even where income barely moves.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_5d.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A grid of 12 small scatterplots, one every 5 years from 1952 to
              2007, each with the same axes, colour, shape, and size encoding as
              the 2007 chart.
              Across the panels, the overall cloud of points shifts upward and
              to the right, showing rising life expectancy and income across
              most countries over time.
              A few panels show individual bubbles that drop backward, visible
              as anomalies against the general upward trend.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Read the panels from left to right, top to bottom, and you can watch
        whole countries move: life expectancy climbs steadily almost everywhere,
        even where income barely changes.
        Every now and then, a bubble drops backwards in a single panel, which
        means something went sharply wrong.
        That's definitely worth investigating, instead of dismissing it as
        noise.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can also change the look of the whole chart using a
        <term>theme</term>.
        Themes control the background, gridlines, fonts, and the other parts of
        the chart that aren't data.
        <c>theme_bw()</c> gives a clean white background with grey gridlines,
        which lots of people find easier to read than the default grey
        background.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp,
                              colour = continent, shape = continent,
                              size = pop)) +
          geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
          scale_x_log10() +
          scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 10)) +
          facet_wrap(~ year) +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy",
               size = "Population") +
          theme_bw()
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        And if you want a proper animation, with countries moving smoothly
        instead of jumping between panels, you can use the <c>gganimate</c>
        package.
        It only needs a couple of extra lines.
        We also add <c>labs(title = "Year: {frame_time}")</c>, so you can see
        which year is being shown at each point in the animation.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # install.packages("gganimate")
        library(gganimate)
        library(dplyr)
ggplot(gapminder, aes(x = gdpPercap, 
                      y = lifeExp,
                      colour = continent, 
                      shape = continent,
                      size = pop,
                      group = country)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
  scale_x_log10() +
  scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 10)) +
  labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", 
       y = "Life expectancy",
       size = "Population") + 
      transition_time(year) +
    labs(title = "Year: {frame_time}")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-5d-animate">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita (log scale) against life expectancy for 142 countries,
          animated by year from 1952 to 2007.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_animate.gif" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A gif animating the 12 small scatterplots from the previous plot
              so that the previous plot transitions from one time point to the
              next seamlessly.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <insight>
        <p>
          Watch the animation carefully.
          In 1977, one country's bubble drops sharply downwards: a sudden fall
          in life expectancy that really stands out against the general upward
          trend.
          And in 1992, it happens again, even more dramatically.
          Before assuming these are errors in the data, remember what we talked
          about in <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>: a value that looks
          implausible isn't always wrong.
          Sometimes, it's the data telling you something real.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can find out which countries these are by filtering the data, and
          looking for unusually low life expectancies in those years:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    library(dplyr)

    # Which country had the lowest life expectancy in 1977?
    gapminder %&gt;%
      filter(year == 1977) %&gt;%
      arrange(lifeExp) %&gt;%
      head(5)
    #&gt; # A tibble: 5 x 6
    #&gt;   country      continent  year lifeExp      pop gdpPercap
    #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;        &lt;fct&gt;     &lt;int&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;int&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
    #&gt; 1 Cambodia      Asia       1977    31.2  6978607      525.
    #&gt; 2 Sierra Leone  Africa     1977    36.8  3140897     1348.
    #&gt; 3 Guinea-Bissau Africa     1977    37.5   745228      765.
    #&gt; 4 Afghanistan   Asia       1977    38.4 14880372      786.
    #&gt; 5 Angola        Africa     1977    39.5  6162675     3009.

    # Which country had the lowest life expectancy in 1992?
    gapminder %&gt;%
      filter(year == 1992) %&gt;%
      arrange(lifeExp) %&gt;%
      head(5)
    #&gt; # A tibble: 5 x 6
    #&gt;   country      continent  year lifeExp      pop gdpPercap
    #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;        &lt;fct&gt;     &lt;int&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;int&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
    #&gt; 1 Rwanda       Africa     1992    23.6  7290203      737.
    #&gt; 2 Sierra Leone Africa     1992    38.3  4260884     1069.
    #&gt; 3 Somalia      Africa     1992    39.7  6099799      927.
    #&gt; 4 Angola       Africa     1992    40.6  8735988     2628.
    #&gt; 5 Liberia      Africa     1992    40.8  1912974      637.
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The 1977 drop is Cambodia, during the Khmer Rouge genocide, which ran
          from 1975 to 1979.
          The 1992 drop is Rwanda, in the years around the Rwandan genocide of
          1994.
          Neither of these is a data error.
          They're both real, terrible events, showing up in the data as a sudden
          reversal against a trend that was moving the other way almost
          everywhere else.
          This is one of the things multidimensional visualisation makes
          possible: an anomaly that could easily be buried in a table of 1,704
          rows becomes obvious as soon as you can see all the countries moving
          together over time.
          And asking whether that anomaly is a mistake or something real is
          exactly the right question.
          Here, the answer is history, and a quick search can explain it.
        </p>
      </insight>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-summary">
      <title>What just happened</title>

      <p>
        So, what just happened?
        We put five variables into one graphic, each one shown with its own
        aesthetic mapping, all in the <em>same</em> visualisation.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-gapminder-dimensions">
        <title>Dimensions added to the Gapminder chart, one at a time</title>

        <tabular halign="left">
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Dimension</cell>
            <cell>Aesthetic</cell>
            <cell>Variable</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>1st &amp; 2nd</cell>
            <cell>x, y position</cell>
            <cell><c>gdpPercap</c>, <c>lifeExp</c></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>3rd</cell>
            <cell>colour &amp; shape</cell>
            <cell><c>continent</c></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>4th</cell>
            <cell>size</cell>
            <cell><c>pop</c></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>5th</cell>
            <cell>facet / animation frame</cell>
            <cell><c>year</c></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Each new variable was added as a new <term>aesthetic mapping</term>.
        Position, colour, shape, size, and facets are all different ways of
        showing information in the same image.
        The elephant study from the start of the chapter used exactly the same
        idea: GPS location, water availability, human modification, sex, and
        time, all held together in one analysis, instead of one table per
        variable.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Bryan, J. <c>gapminder</c> R package, data curated
        by the Gapminder Foundation (<url href="https://www.gapminder.org"
        visual="gapminder.org"/>), based on the work of Hans Rosling.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-visualising-multidimensional">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-challenger">
      <title>The Challenger disaster: when a chart failed</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          On 28 January 1986, the Space Shuttle Challenger exploded 73 seconds
          after launch, and all seven crew members were killed.
          The cause was the failure of rubber O-rings in the rocket boosters.
          These seals became brittle in the cold, and couldn't stop hot gases
          from escaping.
        </p>

        <p>
          The engineers who built the rocket boosters were worried.
          The night before the launch, temperatures were forecast to drop to
          between −3°C and −2°C (26 to 29°F).
          Engineers at Morton Thiokol, the company that made the solid rocket
          boosters, argued that the launch should be delayed.
          They had data from earlier launches showing O-ring damage at low
          temperatures.
          But they didn't get that data across clearly.
          They presented it in tables and engineering diagrams, instead of in a
          visualisation of the relationship between temperature and damage.
          NASA managers approved the launch.
        </p>

        <p>
          In his book <em>Visual Explanations</em> (1997), Edward Tufte argues
          that the engineers had all the data they needed to make the case for a
          delay.
          The problem was that they didn't show it in a way that made the
          pattern visible.
          In this exercise, you'll build the kind of chart that could have been
          shown.
        </p>

        <p>
          The data is in the <c>DAAG</c> package:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
          # install.packages("DAAG")
          library(DAAG)
          library(ggplot2)

          head(orings)
          #&gt;   Temperature Erosion Blowby Total
          #&gt; 1          53       3      2     5
          #&gt; 2          57       1      0     1
          #&gt; 3          58       1      0     1
          #&gt; 4          63       1      0     1
          #&gt; 5          66       0      0     0
          #&gt; 6          67       0      0     0
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Each row is one launch before Challenger.
          <c>Temperature</c> is the launch temperature in degrees Fahrenheit,
          and <c>Total</c> is the total number of O-rings showing damage.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-a">
        <title>Part A: Plot the data</title>

        <statement>
          <p>
            Make a scatter plot with <c>Temperature</c> on the x-axis and
            <c>Total</c> O-ring damage on the y-axis.
            What pattern can you see?
            Is there a relationship between temperature and damage?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <program language="r">
            <code>
            ggplot(orings, aes(x = Temperature, y = Total)) +
              geom_point() +
              labs(x = "Temperature (°F)",
                   y = "Total O-ring damage") +
              theme_bw()
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            There's a clear negative relationship: the colder the launch, the
            more O-ring damage there tends to be.
            Every launch below 66°F had some damage.
            And at 53°F, the total damage was 5, the highest in the dataset.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-b">
        <title>Part B: Add the forecast temperature</title>

        <statement>
          <p>
            On the day of the Challenger launch, the forecast temperature was 26
            to 29°F.
            Add this to your plot as a shaded region, so that anyone looking at
            it can see straight away where the launch temperature sits compared
            with all the previous launches and their O-ring damage.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <hint>
          <p>
            Use <c>xlim()</c> and <c>ylim()</c> to stretch the axes far enough to
            include the forecast temperature, and <c>annotate()</c> with
            <c>geom = "rect"</c> to draw the shaded region.
          </p>
        </hint>

        <solution>
          <program language="r">
            <code>
            ggplot(orings, aes(x = Temperature, y = Total)) +
              geom_point() +
              xlim(25, 85) +
              ylim(0, 6) +
              annotate("rect",
                       xmin = 26, xmax = 29,
                       ymin = 0,  ymax = 6,
                       alpha = 0.3, fill = "red") +
              annotate("text",
                       x = 33, y = 3,
                       label = "Forecast\ntemperature\n26 to 29°F",
                       hjust = 0) +
              labs(x = "Temperature (°F)",
                   y = "Total O-ring damage",
                   title = "O-ring damage by launch temperature",
                   subtitle = "Previous launches and forecast temperature for Challenger") +
              theme_bw()
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            The shaded region sits far to the left of every previous launch.
            The pattern in the existing data (more damage at lower temperatures)
            points straight into the forecast range.
            You don't need any statistical training to see the risk in this
            chart.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-c">
        <title>Part C: What the engineers actually showed</title>

        <statement>
          <p>
            The engineers at Morton Thiokol didn't show a chart like this.
            They presented tables of numbers, and engineering diagrams of how
            O-ring erosion interacted with the secondary O-ring.
            These were detailed and technically accurate, but it was almost
            impossible to see the temperature relationship at a glance.
          </p>

          <figure xml:id="fig-challenger-original-chart">
            <caption>
              One of the charts presented by Morton Thiokol engineers during the
              discussions about whether to launch Challenger.
            </caption>

            <image source="images/ch5_challenger_original.png" width="80%">
              <description>
                <p>
                  A black and white chart titled <q>History of O-Ring Damage in
                  Field Joints (Cont)</q>, from Morton Thiokol.
                  It shows 48 small drawings of rocket boosters in two rows, a
                  pair labelled A and B for each of 24 solid rocket motors.
                  The O-ring temperature in degrees Fahrenheit is written
                  vertically on one booster in each pair, with values such as
                  66, 70, 53, 57, 75, and 81.
                  Shaded marks on a few boosters show where erosion, blowby, or
                  heating damage happened.
                  The boosters are in launch order, so to compare temperature
                  and damage, you have to read each label one at a time.
                </p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </figure>

          <p>
            Put this chart next to your plot from Part B.
            What question is each one answering?
            How easy is it to see the link between temperature and damage in
            each one?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            The original chart is organised launch by launch, with the
            temperature written as a label beside each rocket, instead of placed
            on an axis.
            To see whether colder launches had more damage, you'd have to read
            every label, remember it, and sort the launches in your head.
            Your plot from Part B does that sorting for you: temperature is on
            the x-axis, so the pattern jumps out the moment you look.
          </p>

          <p>
            There's another problem with the charts that were shown, and it's
            about which launches made it onto them at all.
            We come back to this in <xref ref="ex-challenger-missing"/>, once
            we've looked at missing data.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-d">
        <title>Part D: Reflect</title>

        <statement>
          <p>
            This case is sometimes used to argue that better visualisation would
            have prevented the disaster.
            Robison et al. (2002) argue that it was more complicated than that.
            In their view, the engineers did communicate their concerns clearly,
            but organisational and political pressures overrode their
            objections.
          </p>

          <p>
            In two or three sentences: do you think a better chart would have
            changed the outcome?
            What does this case tell you about the relationship between data,
            visualisation, and decision-making?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            There's no single right answer here.
            The chart in Part B makes the relationship between temperature and
            damage obvious, which is a real improvement on what was shown.
            But Robison et al. make an important point: managers knew about the
            engineers' concerns.
            A clearer chart might have made the engineers' argument stronger,
            but there was also organisational pressure to launch, from schedule
            delays, public visibility, and budgets.
            For us, this case shows that a good chart helps people communicate,
            and that it still needs an organisation that takes safety seriously
            enough to listen.
            Better data presentation is necessary, but it isn't always enough.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Tufte, E.R. (1997).
        <em>Visual Explanations: Images and Quantities, Evidence and
        Narrative.</em> Graphics Press.
        Robison, W. et al. (2002).
        <q>Representation and Misrepresentation: Tufte and the Morton Thiokol
        Engineers on the Challenger.</q>
        <em>Science and Engineering Ethics</em>, 8, 59<ndash/>81.
        Data from the <c>DAAG</c> R package.
        Case study adapted from Helveston, J.P. (2019), George Washington
        University,
        <url href="https://p4a.seas.gwu.edu/2019-Fall/challengerExplosion.html"/>,
        CC BY 4.0.
      </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-elephants-variables">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-following-the-giants"/> links each elephant's GPS
          locations to several other variables: vegetation (NDVI), rainfall and
          how variable it was, surface and permanent water, how rugged the
          terrain was, human modification of the landscape, sex, and time.
          The findings we described came from water, rainfall variability, human
          modification, sex, and time.
          Pick one of the variables that didn't come up in those findings
          (vegetation, or terrain ruggedness), and describe in a sentence or two
          what mapping it onto colour, size, or facets might show you that the
          findings we described wouldn't.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-flamingo-dimensions">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Go back to <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c> from <xref ref="ch-intro"/>.
          Build a single plot that shows at least four variables at once (for
          example, bill length and body mass on the axes, species as colour, and
          lake as facets).
          What does this combined view show you that summarising each column
          separately wouldn't?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-gapminder-label">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Adapt the code in <xref ref="ss-gapminder-5d"/> to add a label to the
          animation for a country of your choice.
          How does that country change over the fifty-five years?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-gapminder-1952">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Adapt the code in <xref ref="ss-gapminder-4d"/> to show 1952 instead
          of 2007.
          Compare the two charts.
          Which continents have moved the most in the fifty-five years between
          them, and along which axis?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-too-many-dimensions">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The chart in <xref ref="ss-gapminder-5d"/> already shows five
          variables: two on the x and y axes, then continent as colour and
          shape, population as size, and year as facets (or animation frames).
          Is there a point where adding another variable to the same chart makes
          it harder to read, instead of easier?
          Argue for a specific upper limit, and say what you'd do with a sixth
          or seventh variable, instead of cramming it into the same plot.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>